                    briefing_text_r1:       `Before you decide anything, establish the true baseline. The ECB found that <strong>31% of H1 'sustainable-labelled' loans fail your own taxonomy</strong> — that is greenwashing in regulatory reporting, not a rounding error. Re-screen the €2.1bn honestly to recompute the corrected ESG score, and decide where the committee's first attention goes. The trap: chasing the headline score while two legacy borrowers worth <em>€145M see their blended PD triple from 1.2% to 3.8%</em>. Sustainability scoring is never a substitute for credit-risk management — and weak ESG-risk governance is what triggers the ECB capital add-on.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — The credit deck: approve / decline / price`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The core round. Act as the Credit Committee on a deck of live applications — a €60M cement plant (high emissions, credible electrification plan), a €12M plastics packager (high return, fails screening), a €25M solar cooperative (strong ESG, thin margin) and a €40M agri-processor (weak governance). For each, decide <strong>approve, decline or approve-with-conditions</strong>, and set a <strong>sustainability pricing adjustment within ±60 bps</strong>. Use conditions — covenants, transition milestones, margin ratchets — instead of binary rejection where it adds value. And keep total new origination at or above the <em>€100M floor</em>: a bank that freezes lending has no future to be sustainable about.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Defend the decisions`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Three audiences, three pressures. To the <strong>ECB</strong>: present the re-screened book with documented verification, not promises. To the <strong>CFO</strong>: justify your pricing with deal economics, not slogans — the book must cover its cost of risk-weighted capital. To the <strong>NGO Charter partner</strong>: explain why transition lending to high-emitters is credible, not cover — show the milestone-backed ratchet, not a brochure. And decide the <em>workout path for the €145M arrears exposure</em>: ESG scoring does not provision a loan that has stopped paying.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover: the 2027 mandate path`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Set the forward policy that gets BMC to the Charter targets without repeating the failure. Relationship-Manager bonuses are still tied to <em>volume, not portfolio quality</em> — that is what created this crisis. Redesign the incentive so volume and sustainability are jointly rewarded, set governance so the ESG screen <strong>cannot be bypassed</strong>, and commit a credible green-origination mix target. Project the path from <strong>58 to 70 on the ESG score and 24% to 40% green origination</strong> — a milestone-backed trajectory the supervisory board can defend, not an over-promised number.`,

                    r1_screen_label:        `How do you re-screen the EUR 2.1bn of labelled originations?`,
                    screen_taxonomy:        `Apply the bank's own taxonomy strictly and strip every loan that fails verification`,
                    screen_defend:          `Defend the existing labels to the ECB — most were 'broadly green' in spirit`,
                    screen_relabel:         `Quietly re-label the failing loans 'transition' so the headline green share holds`,
                    r1_priority_label:      `Where do you focus the committee's first attention?`,
                    priority_arrears:       `The €145M arrears exposure — PD has tripled to 3.8%; provisioning risk is live`,
                    priority_score:         `Only the ESG score — the mislabelled loans are the regulator's headline`,
                    priority_profit:        `Only this quarter's profit — the arrears are last year's problem`,
                    r1_rigour_label:        `How rigorously do you document verification evidence for each label? (%)`,
                    r1_rigour_readout:      `Verification evidence on file, not borrower's word:`,

                    r2_cement_label:        `€60M cement plant — high current emissions, credible electrification plan (transition case)`,
                    r2_plastics_label:      `€12M single-use-plastics packager — highly profitable, fails ESG screening`,
                    r2_solar_label:         `€25M community solar cooperative — strong ESG, thin margins, modest collateral`,
                    r2_agri_label:          `€40M agri-processor — established, profitable, weak governance disclosure`,
                    dec_approve:            `Approve clean — book the volume now`,
                    dec_decline:            `Decline — the borrower emits today`,
                    dec_conditions:         `Approve with conditions — sustainability-linked margin ratchet on verified milestones`,
                    dec_conditions_brown:   `Approve with a brown premium and a phase-out covenant`,
                    dec_conditions_green:   `Approve with a green discount and a collateral top-up condition`,
                    dec_approve_green:      `Approve clean — exactly the origination the Charter rewards`,
                    dec_decline_thin:       `Decline — the margin is too thin to bother`,
                    dec_conditions_gov:     `Approve with conditions — governance covenants and disclosure milestones`,
                    dec_decline_gov:        `Decline — the governance gap is disqualifying`,
                    dec_approve_gov:        `Approve clean — it has always paid on time`,
                    r2_spread_label:        `Sustainability pricing adjustment on approved loans (basis points, ±60)`,
                    r2_spread_hint:         `Positive = brown premium widens the spread on weak-ESG borrowers; negative = green discount narrows it. Flat (0) cross-subsidises brown with green and misses the return hurdle.`,

                    r3_ecb_label:           `To the ECB: how do you present the re-screened book?`,
                    ecb_evidence:           `Show the corrected score with documented verification for every retained label`,
                    ecb_promise:            `Promise a corrected score next quarter and ask for time`,
                    ecb_dispute:            `Dispute the finding — argue the desk review misread the taxonomy`,
                    r3_cfo_label:           `To the CFO: how do you justify your pricing?`,
                    cfo_economics:          `Show deal-by-deal economics — each loan covers its risk-weighted cost of capital`,
                    cfo_slogan:             `Lead with the sustainability story; the numbers will follow`,
                    cfo_volume:             `Promise to book more volume to make the return target on aggregate`,
                    r3_ngo_label:           `To the NGO: is transition lending to high-emitters credible or cover?`,
                    ngo_milestones:         `Credible when bound to verified milestones and ratchets — show the cement structure`,
                    ngo_exit:               `Concede the point and pledge to exit all high-emitters entirely`,
                    ngo_dismiss:            `Dismiss the question — lending decisions are commercially confidential`,
                    r3_workout_label:       `Workout path for the €145M arrears exposure (logistics + textiles)`,
                    workout_restructure:    `Restructure with tighter covenants and provision proactively for the rising PD`,
                    workout_ignore:         `Leave it — focus on the score; the borrowers may recover on their own`,
                    workout_firesale:       `Fire-sale both exposures immediately at a steep loss to clean the book`,

                    r4_incentive_label:     `Redesign the Relationship-Manager incentive`,
                    incentive_balanced:     `Joint scorecard — reward volume AND portfolio ESG quality and risk-adjusted return`,
                    incentive_volume:       `Keep bonuses tied to volume — it is what drives origination`,
                    incentive_esgonly:      `Reward ESG score only — quality matters more than growth now`,
                    r4_governance_label:    `Governance so the ESG screen cannot be bypassed`,
                    gov_secondline:         `Mandatory screening with independent second-line sign-off and transition-finance rules`,
                    gov_guidance:           `Issue guidance and trust Relationship Managers to apply it`,
                    gov_none:               `No new controls — the crisis has made everyone careful enough`,
                    r4_mix_label:           `Green / transition origination-mix target to commit to the supervisory board (%)`,
                    r4_mix_hint:            `The Charter requires ≥40% by 2027. A credible, milestone-backed path beats an over-promised number you cannot originate.`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you defend that sustainability lives inside the credit decision, not as a label? (%)`,

                    warn_rigour_range:      `Verification rigour must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_spread_range:      `The sustainability pricing adjustment must be between −60 and +60 basis points.`,
                    warn_mix_range:         `The origination-mix target must be between 20% and 60%.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_taxonomy:      `<strong>Honest re-screening is the only credible start.</strong> Stripping every loan that fails your own taxonomy lowers the headline green share today, but it gives the ECB a defensible corrected score — and that is what protects your CET1 from the add-on.`,
                    coach_r1_defend:        `<strong>'Broadly green in spirit' is exactly what the ECB flagged.</strong> Defending labels you cannot verify is greenwashing in regulatory reporting. The desk review already found 31% fail — arguing the toss now hardens the supervisor against you.`,
                    coach_r1_relabel:       `<strong>Re-labelling failing loans 'transition' to hold the number is the original sin, repeated.</strong> Transition is a verified, milestone-backed category — not a relabelling trick. When the journalist asks for your evidence in three weeks, what do you show?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You re-screened honestly AND went straight at the €145M arrears, where PD has tripled to 3.8%. Sustainability scoring never substitutes for credit-risk management — provisioning that exposure protects the very capital the mandate needs.`,
                    coach_r1_scoreonly:     `<strong>You fixed the label and forgot the loan.</strong> While you re-screened, the logistics borrower can miss a second payment — €145M at a 3.8% PD provisions into real losses that wipe this quarter's margin. ESG scoring is not a substitute for credit risk management.`,
                    coach_r1_profit:        `<strong>Treating the arrears as 'last year's problem' is how the losses crystallise.</strong> And ignoring the score leaves the ECB add-on certain. You optimised nothing — you just deferred two bills that are both coming due.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>A label without documented verification is just a claim.</strong> That is precisely the greenwashing the ECB found. Low evidence rigour means you cannot defend the corrected score — and weak ESG-risk governance is what triggers the +0.4pp add-on.`,
                    coach_r2_cement_cond:   `<strong>That is transition finance done right.</strong> A €60M loan to a high-emitter with a sustainability-linked ratchet on verified electrification milestones is exactly the lending the Charter rewards — you financed the transition instead of excluding the emitter.`,
                    coach_r2_cement_clean:  `<strong>Right deal, missing the structure.</strong> Approving the cement plant clean books the volume, but without milestone conditions you cannot prove the transition is credible — and an unconditioned 'green' label on a high-emitter is what the ECB calls greenwashing.`,
                    coach_r2_cement_decline:`<strong>You declined the transition deal because the borrower emits today.</strong> But transition finance is the point of the mandate — a competitor takes the €60M with a margin ratchet. You avoided a number and missed the lesson: exclusion is not responsibility.`,
                    coach_r2_plastics_cond: `<strong>A brown premium and a phase-out covenant is a defensible middle path</strong> — but a single-use-plastics packager still drags the screen. Make sure the covenant has teeth, or you have simply priced the greenwashing risk rather than removed it.`,
                    coach_r2_plastics_approve:`<strong>Approving the plastics packager clean is margin tunnel vision.</strong> The return is excellent and the loan fails your screen — book it without conditions and you have re-created the exact mislabelling the ECB flagged, for a quarter's margin.`,
                    coach_r2_solar_decline: `<strong>Declining the solar cooperative is margin tunnel vision.</strong> Thin margin, yes — but strong ESG and exactly the green origination you need. Decline your best Charter deals and your score falls while your book shrinks. Price it; don't refuse it.`,
                    coach_r2_agri_approve:  `<strong>Approving the agri-processor clean imports its governance risk.</strong> 'It has always paid on time' is not a control. Conditions — governance covenants and disclosure milestones — turn a weak-governance borrower into a deal you can defend.`,
                    coach_r2_flat:          `<strong>Flat pricing means your solar cooperative subsidises your plastics packager.</strong> Charge one spread regardless of risk or ESG profile and your return on RWA stays below the 1.1% the CFO needs. Pricing is your sharpest tool — you left it in the drawer.`,
                    coach_r2_freeze:        `<strong>You declined too much and origination fell below the €100M floor.</strong> A bank that stops lending has no future to be sustainable about — and your Relationship Managers are quitting. The mandate is a both/and: grow the book AND improve its quality.`,
                    coach_r3_ecb_evidence:  `<strong>Documented verification is what satisfies a supervisor.</strong> Showing the corrected score with evidence for every retained label demonstrates the ESG-risk governance the ECB was testing — and is the strongest defence against the +0.4pp capital add-on.`,
                    coach_r3_ecb_promise:   `<strong>A promise is not a corrected score.</strong> Asking for time after a greenwashing finding reads as more of the same. The ECB tests governance now, not next quarter — buy yourself credibility with evidence, not a deadline extension.`,
                    coach_r3_ecb_dispute:   `<strong>Disputing the finding hardens the supervisor.</strong> The desk review found 31% of labels fail your own taxonomy. Arguing they misread it, with no corrected book to show, makes the Pillar 2 add-on look like the safe call for them.`,
                    coach_r3_cfo_econ:      `<strong>Deal economics is the language the CFO speaks.</strong> Showing that each loan covers its risk-weighted cost of capital proves the mandate and the return hurdle are compatible — sustainability inside the price, not bolted on as a slogan.`,
                    coach_r3_cfo_slogan:    `<strong>The CFO does not lend on slogans.</strong> 'The numbers will follow' is what a return below the 1.1% hurdle sounds like dressed up. Lead with the deal economics or the board will read sustainability as a cost centre.`,
                    coach_r3_cfo_volume:    `<strong>Promising volume to hit the return on aggregate is how this crisis started.</strong> Booking more to average up a thin return is exactly the volume-over-quality reflex the ECB flagged. Price the deals you have; don't chase the ones you shouldn't.`,
                    coach_r3_ngo_exit:      `<strong>Pledging to exit all high-emitters sounds principled but misses the point.</strong> Exclusion just moves the emissions to a less scrupulous lender. The Charter rewards financing a credible transition — that is more impact than a clean-hands divestment.`,
                    coach_r3_ngo_dismiss:   `<strong>'Commercially confidential' is the worst answer to give an NGO partner.</strong> It reads as exactly what the journalist will print. Charter integrity depends on showing your work — the milestone-backed structures are your proof, so use them.`,
                    coach_r3_full:          `<strong>You reconciled all three pressures.</strong> Evidence to the ECB, deal economics to the CFO, and a milestone-backed transition story to the NGO — that is sustainability embedded in the credit decision, defended on its own terms to each audience.`,
                    coach_r3_workout_ignore:`<strong>Ignoring the €145M to fix the score is the classic trap.</strong> The logistics borrower misses a second payment and the exposure provisions €18M — wiping the margin from this quarter's new loans. ESG scoring does not manage default risk; you have to.`,
                    coach_r3_workout_firesale:`<strong>A fire-sale crystallises the loss you were trying to avoid.</strong> Dumping both exposures at a steep discount cleans the book on paper but destroys capital you need for the mandate. A structured restructuring with tighter covenants usually preserves more value.`,
                    coach_r4_inc_balanced:  `<strong>A joint scorecard is the structural fix.</strong> Rewarding volume AND portfolio ESG quality AND risk-adjusted return is what stops Relationship Managers from re-creating this crisis. The incentive, not the memo, is what changes behaviour.`,
                    coach_r4_inc_volume:    `<strong>Keeping bonuses tied to volume guarantees a repeat.</strong> Volume-only incentives are the root cause the ECB letter named — Relationship Managers will book the easy brown deal and label it green again next quarter. You fixed the symptom, not the system.`,
                    coach_r4_inc_esgonly:   `<strong>Rewarding ESG score alone over-corrects.</strong> Strip the volume signal entirely and your Relationship Managers stop originating — the score rises while the book starves. The mandate is a both/and; so is the incentive.`,
                    coach_r4_governance:    `<strong>Second-line sign-off is the guardrail that makes the mandate real.</strong> Mandatory screening with an independent check and explicit transition-finance rules means the ESG screen cannot be quietly bypassed for a high-margin deal — which is precisely what happened in H1.`,
                    coach_r4_gov_guidance:  `<strong>Guidance without a control is a wish.</strong> 'Trust Relationship Managers to apply it' is roughly the policy that produced the 31% mislabelling. Governance has to make bypassing the screen hard, not optional.`,
                    coach_r4_gov_none:      `<strong>'Everyone is careful enough now' is how the next lapse starts.</strong> Memory fades and the volume pressure returns. Without a second-line control the screen gets bypassed again the first time a big brown deal is in a hurry.`,
                    coach_r4_mix_low:       `<strong>A mix target below 40% concedes the Charter.</strong> The commitment is binding by 2027. Committing to less than the mandate tells the board — and the supervisor — that you do not intend to meet it.`,
                    coach_r4_mix_high:      `<strong>An over-promised mix is its own risk.</strong> Committing far above 40% sounds ambitious, but if you cannot originate that much credible green volume you either miss the number or relabel to hit it — straight back to greenwashing. Commit to a path you can defend.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Honest re-screen; arrears risk addressed first.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Defended labels or chased the score over the risk.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Transition financed, priced for risk, floor held.`,
                    sum_r2_frozen:          `Origination below the €100M floor — book frozen.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Mispriced or mis-screened the credit deck.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Evidence to ECB, economics to CFO, restructured arrears.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Weak defence or arrears left unmanaged.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Joint incentive, second-line control, credible mix.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Volume incentive or weak governance — repeat risk.`,

                    insight_made_real:      `Mandate made real: you hit the ESG score, the green-origination mix AND the return hurdle without triggering the capital add-on. Sustainability lived inside the credit decision and the price — you financed credible transitions, priced for risk, and managed the arrears that scoring never fixes.`,
                    insight_margin_tunnel:  `Margin tunnel vision: you earned a great quarter on return, but the ESG score and green share fell further from the mandate. Optimising the income statement shrank the balance sheet you are allowed to run — the ECB add-on is now the binding constraint.`,
                    insight_addon:          `Capital add-on triggered: weak ESG-risk governance let the ECB impose the +0.4pp Pillar 2 add-on — €95M of capital you can no longer lend. Sustainability was a balance-sheet matter all along, not only a reputational one.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short of the 2027 trajectory. Re-examine where you left value on the table — most likely declining a credible transition deal, pricing flat, or letting the arrears run while you fixed the label.`,
                    insight_lowrorwa:       `Return on RWA stayed below the 1.1% CFO hurdle. Flat or under-differentiated pricing cross-subsidises weak-ESG borrowers with strong ones — pricing is the sharpest tool for hitting the return and the mandate together.`,
                    insight_lowesg:         `The ESG score did not reach 70. Honest re-screening and conditioned approvals lift it durably; relabelling and unverified 'green' labels do not — and they are what the supervisor is now watching.`,
                    insight_floor:          `You breached the €100M origination floor at least once. A bank that freezes lending to protect a KPI starves itself of growth and loses its Relationship Managers — the mandate is a both/and, not a brake.`,
                    insight_integrated:     `This is integrated lending: you treated ESG as part of creditworthiness and the loan price, not a separate report. That is the thesis — capital allocated in service of the real economy, with the mandate and the return moving together.`,

                    hint_addon:             `The ECB capital add-on has triggered — €95M of lending capacity is gone. Rebuild ESG-risk governance: honest screening, documented verification and a second-line control are what reverse the supervisor's view.`,
                    hint_floor:             `You are below the €100M origination floor. Stop declining your way to a clean score — approve credible deals with conditions and price them for risk instead of refusing them.`,
                    hint_pricing:           `Return on RWA is below the 1.1% hurdle. Use the sustainability pricing band: a brown premium on weak-ESG borrowers and a measured green discount, not one flat spread for everyone.`,
                    hint_esg:               `Your ESG score has slipped below where you started. Re-screen honestly and use approve-with-conditions — relabelling and unverified labels are exactly what the ECB flagged.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — ESG score, green mix and return all on target with no add-on. Lock it in with the joint incentive and the second-line control so it survives next quarter's volume pressure.`,

